WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.735
-We're back, and my family
is here with us.

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.237
And another development
this year is, Josh,

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:05.604
you and I have started
doing a podcast

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:07.206
called "Family Trips
with the Meyers Brothers."

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.843
-Yeah.
-It's been a delight.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.846
The nicest part about it for me
is we get to spend an hour

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.413
each week talking
with each other.

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:16.449
Here's a -- You guys
were guests on the show.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:17.750
-Yep.
-Which was very fun.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.886
Here's a photo
we have of you guys.

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:22.287
And this is a really --
This is a great picture.

00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:23.956
I showed this to my boys,

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:26.092
and Ashe said,
"What happened to them?"

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.494
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:31.063
Now, do you feel --
We talk about you guys a lot

00:00:31.063 --> 00:00:33.166
on this podcast because we're
talking about family trips

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.201
with our guests, and then
ultimately ours come out.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.704
Do you feel as though you're
being accurately portrayed

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.372
when Josh and I talk
about you on the podcast?

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:40.807
-I don't know.
I don't get that thing

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:43.075
about we spill water everywhere.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:45.178
-Yeah. Josh, do you want
to say what happens

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.479
when they come to your house?

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:51.184
-The kitchen is soaked.
Constantly.

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.586
They -- The sink,
the coffeemaker.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:56.055
The -- I mean, like,
we did redo the kitchen

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:58.091
a couple years ago,
and it used to be small tiles.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.059
-Yeah, it used to be
everything was on an angle.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.694
-Well, now we can
at least just, like,

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:04.430
wipe everything into the sink,
which is a godsend.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.799
-Yeah,
-But, yeah, they're --

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.201
-Now, Dad,
you'll admit you spill.

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:10.537
-No.

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:12.238
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:16.042
[ Applause ]

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:18.111
-I saw it coming a mile away.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.413
-So did I. Guess who didn't.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:23.182
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:26.619
-Right now my mom's going,
"Oh, my God! The spill!"

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.889
-"He spi-- Oh! Ohh! Oh! My God!"

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:32.492
-"What are you doing
in the studio?"

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.026
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:36.062
-Where's that nice picture
we had of him?

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:39.098
-What? Oh, yeah. So this is --
How recent is this picture?

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:42.201
-Two days ago.
-Alright. So two days ago.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:44.337 line:0%
You spill everything you drink.
So this is -- You --

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.306 line:0%
You got a beer, and then
immediately spilled it.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.308 line:0%
-I did. That's --
The spilling is not the part

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:52.411 line:0%
that really troubles me,
but I come off in these things

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.614
very often as being
very temperamental.

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.616
-Yeah.
-You tell a story about me

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:59.119
wanting to destroy a table,

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.053
which I don't remember
the incident.

00:02:01.053 --> 00:02:03.990
I don't doubt that I wanted
to destroy it, but...

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.960
-You bumped into a coffee table
in a Florida hotel lobby,

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.131
and then when it happened,
you raised a fist up

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.500
and started screaming
at the table.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.602
And then when we got
in an elevator,

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:18.805
in a silent lobby -- because
everyone was looking at you --

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.273
Mom said,
"Why would you do that?"

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.908
And you said, "It hurt me.
I wanted to hurt it."

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:26.746
-Yeah. Out of emotion.
Absolutely true.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.614
I just don't remember
that incident.

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:30.950
-You were in a fugue state
of rage.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.919
-That might have been it.
That might have been it.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.922
But, yeah, so I think --
But this is what's important

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:38.024
about what you guys
remember about us.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:42.294
Not the spilling,
which is more recent.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:46.432
But the thing is,
what it shows you is

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.834
that your kids
are paying attention

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:53.439
to every stupid thing you do,

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:56.442
and you never know
which stupid thing you do

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.410
is going to stick with them,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.213
you know, well into later life.

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:04.116
And so -- And you never know.

00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:07.720
And so it appears that we did
plenty of stupid stuff...

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.656
-Yeah.
-...and that you remembered

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:13.025
every single piece of nonsense
that we did.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.228
-I was driving the other day,

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:18.097
and Ashe had a friend
in the back seat.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.699
And I --

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.768
Axel, the 5-year-old,
was being irritating,

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:23.535
and I was getting angry
in the car.

00:03:23.535 --> 00:03:24.937
And it was the most
heartbreaking thing

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.539
'cause Ashe --
I heard Ashe say to his friend,

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.342
"Oh, watch my dad. He's about to
lose it. It's so funny."

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:32.145
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:34.581
And then you realize, like,
oh, it's so ineffective.

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:36.583
Like, the way I react
is so ineffective

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:39.886
that it's already like
a comedy show.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:42.121
-When I got angry,
it was effective.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.522
-Yeah.

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:46.325
For some reason,
they're not afraid of me at all.

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.361
-Yeah.
-One of the nice things

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.596
about the podcast is at the end
of each episode, Josh,

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:54.233
you write a song based on
the interview of the guest,

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.035
and you then sing
and record a song

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.570
that is sort of
an Easter egg for people

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.037
who listen all the way through.
-Yeah.

00:03:59.037 --> 00:04:01.373
So it's a known song.

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:03.342
It'll be sort of
a karaoke version of a song

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:05.678
that I tweak the lyrics.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:09.381
And -- And, yeah.
So I feel like it's a gift

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:12.351
to the people
who came onto the podcast.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.854
I don't think a lot of people
who are guests on podcasts

00:04:15.854 --> 00:04:18.324
then listen to that podcast.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.627
-So we have started sending out
an e-mail that's like,

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:22.328
"Hey, in case
you're not listening,

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:23.996
maybe you want
to listen to this."

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.799
But, like,
Timothy Olyphant called you.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.469
Amy Poehler told you personally.
-Yeah, they like their songs.

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.270
-They like their songs.
-It's very nice.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.005
-I wrote a song
for the Haim sisters.

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.474
I still haven't heard
whether or not they liked it.

00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:37.043
-They're musicians.
-They're musicians.

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.577
So I felt like
I needed to elevate it.

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:41.914
-Yeah. Probably not a great sign
that you haven't heard from 'em.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.749
-I haven't heard from 'em.
-Yeah.

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.518
-Yeah, but we're not --
We don't have each other's info.

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:47.253
-Yeah. That's true.
-Yeah.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:49.389
-Did you guys have a favorite
guest that we've had so far?

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:52.124
-I thought Leslie Jones
was pretty good.

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:53.692
-Leslie Jones is pretty good.
-Pretty good.

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:56.462
And then you had to do the
disclaimer about the language.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.964
-Yes.
-'Cause she does get a...

00:04:57.964 --> 00:05:00.733
-We do want it to be the sort
of podcast you can listen to

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.603
with your family in the car,
with some exceptions.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:04.803
-Yes.

00:05:04.803 --> 00:05:06.739
But that song was so good

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:10.209
because she described a Marriott
that she went to

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.110
that was like a castle.
-Building a castle. Yeah.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.379
-Built like a castle.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:15.982
So then Joshy does the song
from "Misérables" --

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.483
"The Castle in the Clouds."

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.452
I mean, who would think
Leslie Jones --

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:22.088
a "Misérables" song?

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:24.156
I mean, it was just --
-It was a nice fit.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:26.626
-It was a great fit, yeah.
-Dad, do you have a favorite ep?

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:28.060
-Our episode was really good.
-Yeah.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.629
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.565
-It was really --
What was the song for us?

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.099
-Hm.
-"Jack &amp; Diane."

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:35.534
-"Jack &amp; Diane."
-"Jack &amp; Diane."

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.002
-Yeah.
-That's us.

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.138
-Two crazy kids
doing the best that we can.

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.406
-Yeah.

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.975
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.143
-I don't even know what
we're dealing with anymore.

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:46.712
-I'm also very excited.
We are --

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.281
We do an annual trip, as well.
We're gonna go to Pittsburgh.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:51.383
We're gonna hope
the Steelers offense is better.

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:53.619
We're gonna record an episode
at an Airbnb there,

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.321
a special episode
where we're gonna

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:57.223
let our listeners
ask you guys questions.

00:05:57.223 --> 00:05:58.758
So that's gonna be
a lot of fun.

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:00.593
And this -- We've done it
10 years, you guys.

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:01.794
-Yeah. Thanks.
-Well done.

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:04.030
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

